{#-
  DERIVED, not from upstream. Hermes's context_compressor is a conversation
  compactor and ships no observation-level prompt. This template keeps Hermes's
  own discipline -- verbatim quoting of what the task demands, numbered concrete
  actions with explicit outcomes, "Active State" carried as literal state rather
  than narrative -- and applies it to one observation.

  Hermes's "Active Task" field is what makes the derivation work on this axis: it
  already forces the compressor to identify what the next step must act on, which
  is the same question an observation refinement has to answer.
-#}
You are refining a single tool observation for an agent's working context.

The observation is being kept because the agent's next steps depend on it, so this is not free-form summarising: anything a later step will paste into a call, compare against, or branch on has to survive verbatim.

TASK THE AGENT IS WORKING ON:
{{ task }}

ACTIONS AND OBSERVATIONS SO FAR:
{{ history }}

OBSERVATION TO REFINE:
{{ observation }}

Rewrite that observation using this structure.

## Active State
[What the agent must act on next, drawn from THIS observation where this observation
is the evidence: the concrete ids, statuses, counts, paths and error conditions it
establishes. Reference the observation, do not restate its whole payload.]

## Retained Verbatim
[Numbered list of the literals a later call will reuse exactly as written:
identifiers, amounts, timestamps, field names, parameter names, error messages,
exact strings. Format each as: N. VALUE — what it is for]

## Dropped
[What was removed and why it cannot be acted on: schema boilerplate, prompt
echoes, padding, entries already covered above, repeated descriptions. If entries
were dropped from a list, say how many.]

## Refined Observation
[The observation itself, reduced. This is what replaces the original, so it must
stand alone: an agent reading only this must be able to take its next action
without seeing the original.]
